<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Renewable Energy – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Renewable Energy in Panama</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Why Is Renewable Energy Important for Panama?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Express opinions about familiar topics. Interactive: Collaborate verbally in small group projects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can express simple opinions on renewable energy by providing logical reasons and relevant examples (e.g., "I believe solar energy is better because Panama has long hours of sunlight, making it efficient and cost-effective." "I think wind energy is helpful because Penonome has strong, consistent winds, which makes wind turbines effective at generating power.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can collaborate in small groups to choose the best renewable energy option for a specific situation (e.g., "We think wind turbines are good for the countryside in Los Santos because there's a lot of open space and strong winds." "We think hydroelectric power is good for areas near the Chiriqui Viejo River because it can provide consistent energy.")</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can share a personal view on conserving energy (e.g., "We should all unplug devices when they're not in use to save energy and protect the environment.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various images of energy sources (e.g., a solar panel, a wind turbine, a hydroelectric dam, a car exhaust pipe, a coal power plant). Students, in pairs, identify each image verbally and say if it's "renewable" or "non-renewable." Example: "This is a solar panel. It's renewable energy." (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models expressing opinions and providing simple justifications using target grammar and vocabulary. Example: "I believe solar energy is good because it's clean and sustainable. It helps preserve the environment." The teacher focuses on the stress in "renewable," "preservation," and "sustainability." (3 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key terms and opinion expressions.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'renewable,' does it run out?" (Expected answer: "No, it doesn't run out.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'I believe solar energy is good because...', what am I doing?" (Expected answer: "You are giving your opinion and justifying it.")</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher may also practice minimal pairs like /e/ in "pen" and /eɪ/ in "pain" with words like "energy" vs. "maintain" if applicable, though the core focus is on expressing opinions. (2 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Renewable Energy in Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio recording (approx. 1.5-2 min) of a Panamanian teenager discussing the importance of two or three renewable energy sources for Panama. The recording includes opinions, justifications using adverbial clauses, gerunds, and present continuous (e.g., "I think hydropower is great because it provides consistent electricity," "Saving energy is crucial," "Currently, we are investing more in wind turbines.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Explaining environmental benefits and justifying opinions. Students will be exposed to authentic examples of expressing opinions and justifications in context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (verbal response):</b> "What types of renewable energy does the speaker mention?" "What is one main reason the speaker thinks renewable energy is important?" (Students respond verbally to check understanding.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching (verbal matching):</b> The teacher provides a list of renewable energy sources and a list of benefits. Students verbally match the source to its benefit as mentioned in the audio. Example: Teacher says "Solar energy," students say "Clean air and cost-effective because Panama has long hours of sunlight."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using gerunds to describe environmental actions, using the present continuous to describe ongoing actions, and adverbial clauses for justification (e.g., "because," "since"). Vocabulary: <i>energy, solar, wind, hydropower, source, generate, preserve, sustainable, efficient, reduce, consume, invest, save, agree, emit</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Builders):</b> The teacher provides sentence starters and word options on the board. Students work individually or in pairs to verbally complete the sentences, practicing target grammar. (5 min)
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"________ energy is important for the future." (Possible verbs: Save, Saving, To save) → "Saving energy is important for the future."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We are currently ________ more solar panels." (Possible verbs: install, installing, installed) → "We are currently installing more solar panels."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Renewable energy is vital ________ it preserves natural resources." (Possible clauses: but, or, because) → "Renewable energy is vital because it preserves natural resources."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Opinion Chain):</b> Students sit in a circle. One student starts by stating an opinion about a renewable energy source and justifying it (e.g., "I think wind energy is great because it doesn't emit pollution."). The next student must agree or disagree and add another reason, or state an opinion about a different energy source using the target structures. The teacher encourages focus on stress in multisyllabic words like "efficiently" and "environment." (7 min)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Debate Cards):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives two cards: one with a renewable energy source (e.g., "Solar Energy") and another with a problem or challenge (e.g., "High initial cost" or "Intermittent power"). They must discuss and justify why their energy source is still beneficial *despite* the challenge, or propose a solution. They should use phrases like "I agree with you, but...", "I believe that...", "We need to reduce dependence on fossil fuels, increasing solar energy projects. This benefits both the environment and the economy." Peers listen and provide feedback on the clarity of opinions and use of justifications. (8 min)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Renewable Energy in Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Panama's Green Energy Plan" (Small Group Discussion & Presentation)</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a specific scenario for a Panamanian region (e.g., "A rural community in Los Santos with strong winds and open land," "A coastal city in Colón with many sunny days," "A community near a river in Chiriquí"). Their task is to:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Discuss and choose:</b> As a group, discuss and agree on the BEST renewable energy option for their assigned region, considering its benefits and challenges. They must use target vocabulary (e.g., <i>wind, solar, hydropower, efficiency, sustainability, cost-effective</i>) and grammar (gerunds, present continuous, adverbial clauses for justification). Example phrases: "We think wind turbines are good for Los Santos because there's a lot of open space and strong winds." "Hydroelectric power is best for Chiriquí since it can provide consistent energy." "Investing in solar panels is a smart move as it reduces electricity bills."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Prepare a mini-presentation:</b> Prepare a short (1-2 minute) verbal presentation explaining their choice and justifying it with at least two reasons. They should also include a personal view on energy conservation for Panamanians (e.g., "We should all unplug devices when they're not in use to save energy and protect the environment.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Present:</b> Each group presents their "Green Energy Plan" to the class. The teacher reminds them to focus on clear pronunciation, especially of multisyllabic words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This task fosters collaborative interaction, critical thinking, and fluent expression of opinions and justifications related to the theme "Why Is Renewable Energy Important for Panama?" (15 min discussion + 5 min presentations)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students' verbal group presentations from Stage 4. The teacher observes and takes notes using an observation checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Clear and understandable, with appropriate stress on target words (e.g., "renewable," "sustainability").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Incorporates at least 5-7 words from the lesson's vocabulary list (e.g., <i>solar, wind, generate, preserve, efficient, sustainable</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar accuracy:</b> Correct use of gerunds for actions, present continuous for ongoing projects, and adverbial clauses for justification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of opinions and justifications:</b> Clearly expresses opinions and provides logical reasons/examples.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative interaction:</b> Actively participates in group discussion, listens to peers, and contributes to the group's decision.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Verbal or Quick Write):</b> (5 min)</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you learned about renewable energy today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now expressing your opinion about renewable energy in English?" (Students can use a scale: "Not confident," "A little confident," "Confident," "Very confident.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about justifying your opinions in English today, and what helped you overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did working with your group help you to speak more confidently?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this speaking lesson? The group task encouraged active participation and the use of target language. Students were able to express simple opinions and provide some justifications. The modeling of pronunciation and opinion structures was effective.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments/scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with forming complex justifications. For future speaking tasks, providing more sentence starters or a word bank for adverbial clauses could be beneficial. I need to ensure students continue to practice the distinction between /e/ and /eɪ/ through more focused drills. For Lesson 4 (Writing), I will build on these spoken ideas by having students write short paragraphs about their chosen energy plans, emphasizing structured arguments and connecting phrases.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing):</b> In the next lesson, students will transition from speaking to writing. They will take the arguments and justifications they developed verbally today for their "Panama's Green Energy Plan" and transfer them into a structured written paragraph, focusing on composition and appropriate grammar use.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>